(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-drill-myocardial-ischemia"]={"title":"Myocardial Ischemia Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug class is first-line antianginal therapy when there are no contraindications?","choices":["Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are the first-line antianginal therapy in the absence of contraindications. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which beta blocker belongs to the third generation of agents?","choices":["Metoprolol","Carvedilol","Propranolol","Atenolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol and labetalol are the third-generation beta blockers. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which drug is non-cardioselective, so it is avoided in patients with asthma?","choices":["Metoprolol","Atenolol","Propranolol","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-cardioselective beta blockers such as propranolol are to be avoided in asthma. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which antianginal carries the warning to avoid rapid discontinuation?","choices":["Amlodipine","Isosorbide mononitrate","Verapamil","Metoprolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients taking a beta blocker such as metoprolol are taught to avoid rapid discontinuation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which drug can cause nightmares, sexual dysfunction and worsened claudication?","choices":["Propranolol","Amlodipine","Isosorbide dinitrate","Diltiazem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers such as propranolol cause fatigue, sexual dysfunction, nightmares and worsened claudication. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker slows atrioventricular conduction the most?","choices":["Nifedipine","Verapamil","Amlodipine","Felodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil shows the largest fall in atrioventricular conduction, along with lower heart rate and contractility. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker dilates vessels strongly without changing heart rate or atrioventricular conduction?","choices":["Nifedipine","Verapamil","Amlodipine","Diltiazem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amlodipine gives strong vasodilation with heart rate and atrioventricular conduction unchanged. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker should be avoided in its short-acting form?","choices":["Amlodipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting agents are to be avoided, and nifedipine is the example named. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which drug class slows heart rate and is the preferred initial therapy when beta blockers are contraindicated or not tolerated?","choices":["Non-dihydropyridines","Dihydropyridines","Long-acting nitrates","Short-acting nitrates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines are the initial therapy when beta blockers cannot be used; dihydropyridines are added to a beta blocker. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which drug class is listed as contraindicated with a heart rate below 60 beats per minute, acute heart failure or an ejection fraction below 40 percent?","choices":["Dihydropyridines","Non-dihydropyridines","Long-acting nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low heart rate, acute heart failure and low ejection fraction are contraindications for the non-dihydropyridines. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which drug is placed under the tongue or sprayed for acute relief of angina?","choices":["Isosorbide mononitrate","Amlodipine","Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting nitroglycerin, as a sublingual tablet or spray, relieves acute symptoms and prevents effort-induced angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which drug must never be combined with nitrates because the result can be severe hypotension, myocardial infarction or stroke?","choices":["Metoprolol","Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Sildenafil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sildenafil, tadalafil and vardenafil inhibit phosphodiesterase type 5 and must not be used with nitrates. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which drug class blocks phosphodiesterase type 5, the enzyme that breaks down cyclic guanosine monophosphate, making its combination with nitrates dangerous?","choices":["Phosphodiesterase inhibitors","Calcium channel blockers","Cardioselective beta blockers","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sildenafil, tadalafil and vardenafil inhibit phosphodiesterase type 5, which breaks down cyclic guanosine monophosphate. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which drug class is avoided in aortic valve stenosis and obstructive cardiomyopathy because of the risk of hypotension?","choices":["Beta blockers","Nitrates","Calcium channel blockers","Fibrinolytics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aortic valve stenosis and obstructive cardiomyopathy are listed nitrate contraindications because of hypotension; phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors are the other. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which drug class is worn as a patch or ointment 12 hours on and 12 hours off to limit tolerance?","choices":["Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Antiplatelet drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting nitrate patches and ointment follow a 12-hours-on, 12-hours-off schedule to limit tachyphylaxis. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which drug class does NOT relieve angina symptoms but may help prevent progression of coronary artery disease?","choices":["Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These inhibitors barely change myocardial oxygen consumption and do not relieve angina, but may slow coronary artery disease. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which drug is recommended for patients who are allergic to aspirin?","choices":["Clopidogrel","Enoxaparin","Heparin","Alteplase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clopidogrel is as efficacious as aspirin in secondary prevention and is recommended when aspirin causes allergy. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which drug is chewed and swallowed at the first signs of chest pain in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Clopidogrel","Aspirin","Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is chewed and swallowed at the first signs of chest pain and reduces mortality and reinfarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which drug is contraindicated by allergy, a recent gastrointestinal bleed or a recent intracranial hemorrhage?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol","Aspirin","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Allergy, recent gastrointestinal bleeding and recent intracranial hemorrhage are the aspirin contraindications. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which drug used in acute coronary syndrome relieves chest pain but shows no mortality benefit?","choices":["Aspirin","Metoprolol","Alteplase","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates give only relief of chest pain, with no mortality benefit. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which drug is added for chest pain that does not respond to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Morphine","Nitroglycerin","Aspirin","Metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Morphine, an opioid analgesic, manages chest pain unresponsive to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic forms a stable 1:1 complex with plasminogen to convert it to plasmin?","choices":["Alteplase","Streptokinase","Reteplase","Tenecteplase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Streptokinase forms a stable 1:1 complex with plasminogen, exposing the catalytic site that makes plasmin. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic causes fever, chills and skin rash, making prior exposure a reason to avoid it?","choices":["Alteplase","Reteplase","Streptokinase","Tenecteplase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fever, chills and rash occur mainly with streptokinase, and prior exposure or an allergic reaction to it contraindicates reuse. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic is a recombinant protein made in Escherichia coli cells?","choices":["Alteplase","Tenecteplase","Streptokinase","Reteplase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reteplase (Retavase) is produced recombinantly in Escherichia coli cells. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic is the tissue plasminogen activator sold as Activase?","choices":["Alteplase","Reteplase","Tenecteplase","Streptokinase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alteplase (Activase) is the recombinant tissue plasminogen activator that directly activates fibrin-bound plasminogen. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which drug class is contraindicated by aortic dissection and by serious head or facial trauma?","choices":["Nitrates","Fibrinolytics","Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection and serious head or facial trauma are among the fibrinolytic contraindications. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which drug is NOT recommended in non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome because bleeding risk outweighs benefit?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Aspirin","Alteplase","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrinolytics such as alteplase are not recommended in non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which platelet-directed drug class is used before percutaneous coronary intervention and as clot prophylaxis with stent placement?","choices":["Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors","Heparins","Fibrinolytics","P2Y12 receptor antagonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. P2Y12 receptor antagonists are used before percutaneous coronary intervention and as clot prophylaxis with a stent. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker is the alternative to a beta blocker when left ventricular function is decreased?","choices":["Amlodipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With decreased left ventricular function, a beta blocker is first line and amlodipine is the calcium channel blocker alternative. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which drug class is first line for angina in a patient with bradycardia or atrioventricular block?","choices":["Non-dihydropyridines","Dihydropyridines","Beta blockers","Antiplatelet drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With bradycardia or atrioventricular block, dihydropyridines are first line and long-acting nitrates the alternative. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which antianginal's patient education includes a warning about constipation?","choices":["Metoprolol","Nitroglycerin","Verapamil","Isosorbide mononitrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Education for calcium channel blockers such as verapamil includes dizziness and constipation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which drug class should be avoided in variant (Prinzmetal) angina because it may worsen symptoms?","choices":["Calcium channel blockers","Nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Beta blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In variant angina, calcium channel blockers and nitrates reduce symptoms, but beta blockers may worsen them. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
